# Saving and Loading Game States in a 3D Engine: A C++ Serialization and Deserialization Guide

In modern 3D game engines, saving and loading game states is an essential feature for both gameplay continuity and development workflows. Whether it’s a fully realized open-world adventure or a physics-driven simulation, the ability to serialize complex scenes—complete with textures, meshes, and entity states—into a file, and later reconstruct them exactly, is vital. This tutorial walks through designing a C++ system for serializing and deserializing custom data structures, with special attention paid to binary formats for textures and meshes.

---

## Understanding the Data Model

Before writing any code, define the data you need to preserve. In a typical 3D engine, a game state might include:

- **Entity metadata**: position, rotation, scale, and unique identifiers.
- **Texture data**: raw pixel buffers, resolution, and format (e.g., RGBA8).
- **Mesh data**: vertex buffers, index buffers, normals, UV coordinates.
- **Scene graph**: hierarchical relationships between entities.

A common approach is to encapsulate these into C++ structs or classes:

```cpp
struct Texture {
    uint32_t width;
    uint32_t height;
    std::vector<uint8_t> pixelData; // Binary buffer
};

struct Mesh {
    std::vector<float> vertices;
    std::vector<uint32_t> indices;
    std::vector<float> normals;
    std::vector<float> uvs;
};

struct Entity {
    std::string name;
    float position[3];
    float rotation[4];
    float scale[3];
    Texture texture;
    Mesh mesh;
};
```

---

## Designing a Binary Serialization Format

Binary serialization is preferred for performance and storage efficiency. A simple format might consist of:

1. **Header**: magic number, version, and total entity count.
2. **Entity Blocks**: fixed-size metadata followed by variable-size texture and mesh sections.

For example:

```
[Header]
[Entity #1 Metadata]
[Texture Binary]
[Mesh Binary]
[Entity #2 Metadata]
...
```

---

## Implementing Serialization in C++

Serialization involves writing object data into a stream in a defined order. Using standard C++ I/O:

```cpp
void serializeTexture(std::ofstream& out, const Texture& tex) {
    out.write(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(&tex.width), sizeof(tex.width));
    out.write(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(&tex.height), sizeof(tex.height));
    uint32_t dataSize = static_cast<uint32_t>(tex.pixelData.size());
    out.write(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(&dataSize), sizeof(dataSize));
    out.write(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(tex.pixelData.data()), dataSize);
}

void serializeMesh(std::ofstream& out, const Mesh& mesh) {
    auto writeVector = [&](const auto& vec) {
        uint32_t size = static_cast<uint32_t>(vec.size());
        out.write(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(&size), sizeof(size));
        out.write(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(vec.data()), size * sizeof(vec[0]));
    };
    writeVector(mesh.vertices);
    writeVector(mesh.indices);
    writeVector(mesh.normals);
    writeVector(mesh.uvs);
}

void serializeEntity(std::ofstream& out, const Entity& entity) {
    uint32_t nameLen = static_cast<uint32_t>(entity.name.size());
    out.write(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(&nameLen), sizeof(nameLen));
    out.write(entity.name.data(), nameLen);
    out.write(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(entity.position), sizeof(entity.position));
    out.write(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(entity.rotation), sizeof(entity.rotation));
    out.write(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(entity.scale), sizeof(entity.scale));
    serializeTexture(out, entity.texture);
    serializeMesh(out, entity.mesh);
}
```

---

## Implementing Deserialization in C++

Deserialization reverses the process, reading from a stream and reconstructing objects:

```cpp
Texture deserializeTexture(std::ifstream& in) {
    Texture tex;
    in.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(&tex.width), sizeof(tex.width));
    in.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(&tex.height), sizeof(tex.height));
    uint32_t dataSize;
    in.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(&dataSize), sizeof(dataSize));
    tex.pixelData.resize(dataSize);
    in.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(tex.pixelData.data()), dataSize);
    return tex;
}

Mesh deserializeMesh(std::ifstream& in) {
    Mesh mesh;
    auto readVector = [&](auto& vec) {
        uint32_t size;
        in.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(&size), sizeof(size));
        vec.resize(size);
        in.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(vec.data()), size * sizeof(vec[0]));
    };
    readVector(mesh.vertices);
    readVector(mesh.indices);
    readVector(mesh.normals);
    readVector(mesh.uvs);
    return mesh;
}

Entity deserializeEntity(std::ifstream& in) {
    Entity entity;
    uint32_t nameLen;
    in.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(&nameLen), sizeof(nameLen));
    entity.name.resize(nameLen);
    in.read(&entity.name[0], nameLen);
    in.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(entity.position), sizeof(entity.position));
    in.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(entity.rotation), sizeof(entity.rotation));
    in.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(entity.scale), sizeof(entity.scale));
    entity.texture = deserializeTexture(in);
    entity.mesh = deserializeMesh(in);
    return entity;
}
```

---

## Integrating Into the Engine

Once serialization and deserialization functions are in place, integrate them into your save/load workflow:

- **Save Game**: Traverse the scene graph, collect all active entities, and write them to a `.sav` file using the serialization functions.
- **Load Game**: Read the file, reconstruct all entities, and insert them back into the scene graph.

It’s good practice to include a version number in the header to handle format changes gracefully.

---

## Conclusion

By defining a clear binary layout and implementing robust serialization/deserialization routines in C++, you can reliably save and restore complex game states. This approach ensures textures and meshes retain their fidelity and that entity transformations and hierarchies are preserved exactly, enabling seamless gameplay continuity and efficient asset management in your 3D engine.

