# Streaming Your Desktop Screen to a Web Browser with WebRTC

WebRTC has emerged as a powerful technology for enabling real-time communication between browsers without requiring additional plugins. Among its many capabilities is the ability to capture and stream your desktop screen directly to another user via a secure peer-to-peer connection. This can be useful for demonstrations, presentations, remote collaboration, or simply sharing visual content in real time. In this post, we’ll walk through the process of setting up a Node.js server, using the `getDisplayMedia` API for screen capture, and establishing a secure streaming session.

---

## Setting Up the Node.js Signaling Server

Before you can stream your screen to another browser, you need a signaling mechanism to exchange connection metadata between peers. WebRTC itself doesn’t define signaling, so you can implement it using WebSockets or similar technologies.

1. **Initialize the Project**  
   Create a new directory and initialize a Node.js project:
   ```bash
   mkdir screen-share
   cd screen-share
   npm init -y
   npm install express ws
   ```

2. **Create the Server Script**  
   Use Express to serve static HTML/JavaScript files, and `ws` (WebSocket) to handle signaling:
   ```javascript
   const express = require('express');
   const { Server } = require('ws');
   const app = express();
   const server = app.listen(3000);
   const wss = new Server({ server });

   app.use(express.static('public'));

   wss.on('connection', ws => {
     ws.on('message', message => {
       wss.clients.forEach(client => {
         if (client !== ws && client.readyState === client.OPEN) {
           client.send(message);
         }
       });
     });
   });
   ```
   This server listens on port 3000, serves static files from a `public` directory, and relays WebSocket messages between connected clients.

---

## Capturing the Screen with `getDisplayMedia`

Modern browsers support the `navigator.mediaDevices.getDisplayMedia()` API, which allows you to capture video (and optionally audio) from your display or application windows.

1. **HTML Setup**  
   In `public/index.html`:
   ```html
   <!DOCTYPE html>
   <html>
   <head>
     <title>Screen Share</title>
   </head>
   <body>
     <button id="startShare">Start Screen Share</button>
     <video id="remoteVideo" autoplay playsinline></video>
     <script src="client.js"></script>
   </body>
   </html>
   ```

2. **Client Script**  
   In `public/client.js`:
   ```javascript
   const ws = new WebSocket(`ws://${location.host}`);
   const peer = new RTCPeerConnection();
   const remoteVideo = document.getElementById('remoteVideo');

   peer.ontrack = event => {
     remoteVideo.srcObject = event.streams[0];
   };

   ws.onmessage = async ({ data }) => {
     const message = JSON.parse(data);
     if (message.offer) {
       await peer.setRemoteDescription(message.offer);
       const answer = await peer.createAnswer();
       await peer.setLocalDescription(answer);
       ws.send(JSON.stringify({ answer }));
     } else if (message.answer) {
       await peer.setRemoteDescription(message.answer);
     } else if (message.candidate) {
       await peer.addIceCandidate(message.candidate);
     }
   };

   peer.onicecandidate = event => {
     if (event.candidate) {
       ws.send(JSON.stringify({ candidate: event.candidate }));
     }
   };

   document.getElementById('startShare').onclick = async () => {
     const stream = await navigator.mediaDevices.getDisplayMedia({ video: true, audio: true });
     stream.getTracks().forEach(track => peer.addTrack(track, stream));
     const offer = await peer.createOffer();
     await peer.setLocalDescription(offer);
     ws.send(JSON.stringify({ offer }));
   };
