This script connects to the signaling server via WebSockets, captures the screen when the user clicks “Start Screen Share,” and sends the video stream through a WebRTC peer connection.

---

## Security and Permissions

Because screen sharing can expose sensitive information, browsers enforce strict permission controls. The user must explicitly grant permission to share their display, and the session must originate from a secure context (HTTPS or `localhost`). If you plan to deploy this beyond a local network, ensure you have an SSL certificate and serve your application over HTTPS.

---

## Conclusion

By combining a Node.js signaling server, the `getDisplayMedia` API, and WebRTC’s peer-to-peer streaming capabilities, you can create a robust, real-time screen sharing solution that works directly in modern browsers. This setup avoids third-party plugins, leverages secure connections, and provides flexibility for integrating additional features such as chat or file transfer. Once established, you can adapt the workflow for a variety of real-time visualization and collaboration scenarios.

